Subject: Check a CronJob pod template in the example consumption VAP

Why: the example policy matches CronJobs but reads `spec.template`, which a CronJob does not have, so it falls back to the CronJob's own spec and admits a CronJob that targets the restricted ComputeClass. The pod template is at `spec.jobTemplate.spec.template.spec`.
Local-Issue: #2694
Upstream-Issue: none filed yet
Retire-When: upstream's `podSpec` variable reads `spec.jobTemplate.spec.template.spec` for a CronJob

diff --git a/assets/restrict-computeclass-usage-vap.yaml b/assets/restrict-computeclass-usage-vap.yaml
--- a/assets/restrict-computeclass-usage-vap.yaml
+++ b/assets/restrict-computeclass-usage-vap.yaml
@@ -35,12 +35,12 @@ spec:
       resources: ["jobs", "cronjobs"]
   variables:
   - name: podSpec
-    # Pods carry spec; controllers carry spec.template.spec (CronJob nests one level deeper).
+    # Pods carry spec; controllers carry spec.template.spec; a CronJob has no spec.template
+    # and keeps its pod template at spec.jobTemplate.spec.template.spec, so test it first.
     expression: >-
-      has(object.spec.template) ?
-        (has(object.spec.template.spec) ? object.spec.template.spec :
-          object.spec.jobTemplate.spec.template.spec) :
-        object.spec
+      has(object.spec.jobTemplate) ? object.spec.jobTemplate.spec.template.spec :
+      has(object.spec.template) ? object.spec.template.spec :
+      object.spec
   - name: hasForbiddenNodeSelectorOrAffinity
     expression: >-
       (has(variables.podSpec.nodeSelector) &&
